# A selector list has no meaning here — REFUSED

No `.expected`, so the front gate requires this to be refused.

`import a.{b, c}` imports two names and nothing else, which is a statement about NAMESPACING —
and Tier 0 has none: an import brings the whole module either way. Accepting the spelling would
mean accepting a promise the language cannot keep, so it is refused BY NAME rather than parsed
into something that quietly does more than it says.

```scalascript
import credential.{credentialEnv, credentialFile}

println("unreachable")
```
